@title Message Queue for Order Routing
@subtitle Fulfillment team decision page for the architecture review on 2026-10-21

# Context

The new order-routing service expects 1800 messages per second at peak and 400 on average. Messages are under 8 KB. At-least-once delivery is acceptable, and ordering is needed only per order.

# Options

- SQS
- Kafka (Amazon MSK)
- RabbitMQ (Amazon MQ)

# Comparison

table = Option,Ordering,Throughput ceiling,Ops burden,Replay,Monthly cost; SQS,FIFO per message group,very high,none,no,$310; Kafka,per partition,very high,high,yes up to retention,$1,450; RabbitMQ,per queue,medium,medium,no,$690

data cost = option,usd; SQS,310; Kafka,1450; RabbitMQ,690

chart bar cost unit=$ labels title="Estimated monthly cost"

# Pros and cons

### SQS

**Pros**

- Fully managed and scales automatically
- No broker capacity to plan

**Cons**

- No replay
- 256 KB message limit

### Kafka

**Pros**

- Replay and stream processing
- Retains events for later consumers

**Cons**

- Needs partition planning
- Broker upgrades are our job

### RabbitMQ

**Pros**

- Flexible routing
- Familiar to most backend engineers

**Cons**

- We would own capacity planning
- Throughput ceiling is well below our peak

# Recommendation

! Recommend SQS with FIFO queues keyed by order ID. It meets the ordering rule, scales to peak without capacity work, and costs the least of the three.

We will revisit this if the analytics team needs event replay in 2027.
